# @@ W9-4C-INPUT @@
# Input for ordinary programs (NEXT_STEPS item 5, M6b): buffered stdin, lines,
# words, decimal parsing and argv — the tio/ttext logic, re-cut for the prelude
# (no →x/←x, X/Y frames, raw atoms, masked inputs, tagged results).  LONG names
# only: no single-byte name is left, and the lowercase letters are the user's.
#   ‹getc›     -> b          next stdin byte 0..255, or -1 at end of input
#   ‹readall›  -> list       the rest of stdin as a list of byte codes
#   ‹readline› -> list f     the next line without its '\n'; f 0 = got a line,
#                            1 = end of input and nothing read (zero-is-true)
#   ‹words›    list -> list  split on runs of ASCII whitespace (9-13, 32) into
#                            a list of code lists; never an empty word
#   ‹num›      list -> n     skip leading whitespace, optional + or -, then
#                            decimal digits up to the first non-digit; no
#                            digits -> 0; wraps mod 2^64 like every add
#   ‹ints›     -> list       every word of stdin as an integer, the result of
#                            ‹readall›‹words›′‹num›€ (a non-numeric word counts
#                            as 0) but scanned straight from the buffer (W12-P1)
#   ‹readbytes› -> block     the rest of stdin as a “…“-layout byte block
#                            [len:u32][bytes] (W12-P1)
#   ‹args›     -> list       argv[1..] as a list of code lists (0x4F0040)
# All of them share ONE input buffer, so they interleave freely; the raw `(`
# does not go through it and must not be mixed with them.  Buffer state lives
# in a heap block whose raw address is the cell 0x4F0050 (5177424, REGISTRY §3),
# made on first use: [pos][len][8192 bytes][8 spare bytes] (1027 cells; the
# spare cell, W12-P1, holds ‹ints›'s sentinel byte after the data and lets
# ‹readbytes› copy the data a whole cell at a time).  A failed read(2) counts as end of
# input.  Names starting ‹_in› are reserved for these words' helpers.
# ‹getc›: a leaf (no scratch, no frame).  Refill when pos >= len; on a read of
# <= 0 bytes return -1 and leave pos/len as they are, so the next call reads
# again.  Otherwise bump pos and return the byte at S+16+pos-1.  W11-1C: each
# refill calls ‹flush› first, so a prompt printed before a read is visible.
