# Text words on CODE LISTS (one byte code per cell, what ⊃ and ‹readall› make).
# Every word that returns a list returns a NEW one and never writes its input.
# None of them writes a tag itself: each result is allocated with R (n R is a
# tagged list of length n, header already set) and then overwritten through
# the masked address, so the tag value lives only in R (W11-1A moves it).
# Flags follow ∊ and T: zero-is-true, so `‹startswith›[…]` runs on a match.
#   ‹fmt›        n -> list       signed decimal, `-` first when n < 0; INT64_MIN
#                                works (its \neg is itself and / % are unsigned)
#   ‹hex›        n -> list       lowercase hex, no prefix; n is taken as UNSIGNED
#                                64-bit (two's complement): -1 -> 16 f's, like
#                                Rust's {:x}; 0 -> "0"
#   ‹parsehex›   list -> n       skip leading whitespace, one optional + or -,
#                                then 0-9 a-f A-F up to the first other byte (no
#                                0x prefix: "0x1f" stops at x and is 0); no digits
#                                -> 0; wraps at 64 bits, so ‹hex›‹parsehex› is
#                                the identity on every int.  Like ‹num›, bad
#                                input never errors
#   ‹split›      list sep -> ll  split on every cell equal to sep (one element,
#                                ≡ equality), keeping empty fields: k seps give
