# === W11-2B dict and set ===
# Hash dict and set, keyed by ints or (nested) lists, compared with ≣.
#   ‹hash›  v -> h        FNV-1a 64-bit.  An int: its 8 bytes, low byte first.
#                         A list: from the FNV basis, each element's 8 bytes in
#                         turn, an inner list standing as the 8 bytes of ITS
#                         ‹hash› — so ≣-equal values hash equal (and [5] hashes
#                         like 5; ≣ tells them apart)
#   ‹dict›  -> d          a new empty dict
#   ‹put›   d k v -> d    set k to v (insert, or overwrite in place)
#   ‹get›   d k -> v      the value; a missing k throws `key not found`
#   ‹has›   d k -> f      0 if k is present, else 1 (zero-is-true, like ∊)
#   ‹del›   d k -> d      remove k (no-op when absent)
#   ‹keys›  d -> list     the live keys, in insertion order (a key deleted and
#                         put again goes to the end)
#   ‹vals›  d -> list     their values, in the same order
#   ‹set›   -> s          a dict whose values are all 0;  ‹add›  s k -> s
# d is UPDATED IN PLACE: ‹put› ‹del› ‹add› return the same d, so `‹put›_` is
# fine.  A key list is stored by reference (not copied): changing or freeing
# it while it is a key breaks the dict.  d is a tagged 6-cell list (≢d is 6,
# not the count — use ‹keys›≢):
#   d[0] index table (raw A block, cap cells)   d[1] cap (a power of two, >= 8)
#   d[2] 64 - log2(cap) (the hash shift)        d[3] entries (raw A block)
#   d[4] used (entries appended)                d[5] live (the count)
# A CPython-style compact dict: entries are appended in insertion order as
# triples [h' key value] (h' = ‹hash›>>1, never negative; -1 marks a deleted
# entry), and the index table holds entry number + 1 (0 = empty slot), found
# by linear probing from the top log2(cap) bits of h' * 0x9E3779B97F4A7C15.  A
# deleted entry's slot stays as a tombstone.  When used reaches cap/2 the
# entries are compacted into new blocks, at 2*cap when live >= cap/4 (else the
# same cap), the index is rebuilt and the old blocks go back via ⌫ — so the
# table's load (tombstones included) never passes 1/2.
