# === W11-3C checked arithmetic ===
# ‹cadd› ‹csub› ‹cmul›  a b -> a op b   Rust-style checked 64-bit signed ops:
# the wrapped result when it is exact, else `“integer overflow“U‹throw›` (exit
# 1, or caught by ‹catch›, W11-3A).  The bare + - * keep wrapping.
# SCALAR ONLY: no shape test and no list dispatch — the raw atoms ﹢ ﹣ ﹡ ÷ ≺
# do the work, so an integer whose top 16 bits are 0x7FFF (e.g. INT64_MAX) is
# a number here, and a list's tagged address is just a 64-bit integer.
# Overflow tests (r = the wrapped result):
#   add: (a^r)&(b^r) < 0      both operands differ in sign from r
#   sub: (a^b)&(a^r) < 0      operands differ in sign and r left a's sign
#   mul: a≠0 and (r÷a ≠ b or (a = -1 and b = INT64_MIN)); a = 0 never
#        overflows (and skips the divide).  The extra clause is needed because
#        ÷ defines INT64_MIN÷-1 = INT64_MIN, which would pass the check.
# Scratch: a 5180288, b 5180296, r 5180304 (W11-3C's REGISTRY §3.1 slot).
# Leaves: nothing but the failure path calls a word, so the global cells are
# safe inside a map or a ‹catch›.
