# === W11-4B test words ===
# ‹assert›  f msg ->               PASS iff f is exactly -1, the true of the
#   comparison atoms (≡ ≣ ≺ ≻ < » …), so `a b≡“msg“⊃‹assert›` reads as it
#   says.  Anything else fails — 0, and also 1 and every zero-is-true flag
#   (∊ ‹has› ‹startswith› T): those never pass by accident; write `0≡` first
#   to test one (`l x∊0≡“has x“⊃‹assert›`).
# ‹expect›  actual expected msg -> PASS iff actual ≣ expected (structural, so
#   ints, lists and nested lists alike).
# msg is a code list (`“text“⊃`).  A failure prints, through the buffered
# printers (stdout, in order with the program's own output), one line
#   FAIL: msg                                  (‹assert›)
#   FAIL: msg: expected E got A                (‹expect›; E A as ‹_4b_show›)
# adds 1 to the failure count (REGISTRY §3.1 cell 5180416) and carries on.
# EXIT STATUS: the first failure swaps the 1C flush hook (5179792) for
# ‹_4b_exit›, keeping the old value in 5180424.  ‹_4b_exit› calls that old
# hook (‹flush›) and then looks at the byte its call returns to: the hook is
# called from fixed templates (tools/mkblob2._FLUSH_CALL), so 0x31 (`xor
# edi,edi`) is the end-of-program exit(0) — make it exit(1) — and 0x5F (`pop
# rdi`) is `.` — turn a status 0 on the data stack into 1 (any other status is
# kept).  `)` `(` (0x6A) and the divide-by-zero exit (0x48) just flush.  So a
# test file needs no closing word; a program that never fails never installs
# the hook and pays nothing.  run2's W11-4B block checks both exit paths.
# ‹_4b_show›  v ->   an int as Ṅ; a list as `[a b c]` (the inner elements by ⍕,
#   the last one by ‹_4b_show›, so no trailing space); `[]` when empty.
# Cells: 5180416 failures · 5180424 saved hook · 5180432 actual · 5180440
# expected · 5180448 msg · 5180456 rbp in ‹_4b_exit›.  No swap (`\\`) here:
# codepage.py decode -m prints the swap byte as a bare `\`, so the prelude's
# decode/encode round trip (test/gtools.sh) only holds without it.
:‹_4b_show›"T["≢[_“[]“🗲^]“[“🗲""≢⊖↑"⍕⌫"≢⊖⊇‹_4b_show›“]“🗲^]Ṅ;
